const title = "Guangzhou Travel Guide: Food Capital of China";
const description = "Explore Guangzhou — China's food capital with world-famous dim sum, Cantonese culture, and easy access to Hong Kong.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="th">
 <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
 <header class="mb-12">
 <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">🏙️ Guangzhou</p>
 <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">Guangzhou Travel Guide: Food Capital of China</h1>
 <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">Explore Guangzhou — China's food capital with world-famous dim sum, Cantonese culture, and easy access to Hong Kong.</p>
 <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500"><span>📅 อัปเดตล่าสุด: มิถุนายน 2026</span><span>⏱️ 7 min read</span></div>
 </header>

 <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-green-50 to-teal-50 border-l-4 border-green-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-900 mb-4">⚡ Quick Answer</h2>
 <p class="text-lg text-green-800 leading-relaxed">If you eat to live in Beijing, you <strong>live to eat in Guangzhou</strong>. This southern city is widely regarded as <strong>China's food capital</strong>, and the cradle of Cantonese cuisine — arguably the most famous Chinese cuisine in the world. Beyond the food, Guangzhou offers subtropical greenery, a relaxed pace, and proximity to both Shenzhen and Hong Kong.</p>
 </div>

 <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 ในคู่มือนี้</h3>
 <ul class="space-y-3">
 <li><a href="#why-visit" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🥟 Why Visit Guangzhou?</a></li>
 <li><a href="#how-many-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">📅 How Many Days Do You Need?</a></li>
 <li><a href="#attractions" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🎯 Top Attractions</a></li>
 <li><a href="#getting-around" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🚇 Getting Around</a></li>
 <li><a href="#where-to-stay" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🏨 Where to Stay</a></li>
 <li><a href="#what-to-eat" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🍜 What to Eat</a></li>
 </ul>
 </nav>

 <h2 id="why-visit" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🥟 Why Visit Guangzhou?</h2>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-12">Guangzhou has been a trading port for over 1,000 years. It's where Cantonese cuisine — dim sum, roasted meats, slow-cooked soups — was perfected and exported worldwide. The city has a subtropical climate (warm year-round), a vibrant riverfront, and a distinctly Cantonese culture that feels different from the north. It's also the gateway to the Pearl River Delta: Shenzhen is 30 minutes by high-speed rail, and Hong Kong is about an hour.</p>

 <h2 id="how-many-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">📅 How Many Days Do You Need?</h2>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">
 <li><strong>2–3 days (standard):</strong> Dim sum, Canton Tower, Shamian Island, and a food tour</li>
 <li><strong>4–5 days (comfortable):</strong> Add Chimelong Safari Park, Kaiping Diaolou, or cross to Shenzhen</li>
 <li><strong>5+ days (extended):</strong> Combine with Shenzhen and Hong Kong for a complete Pearl River Delta trip</li>
 </ul>

 <!-- ==================== ATTRACTIONS ==================== -->
 <h2 id="attractions" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🎯 Top Attractions</h2>

 <div class="flex flex-col md:block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
 <div class="order-2 md:float-left md:mr-8 md:mb-4 md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/canton-tower.jpg" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="order-1">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Canton Tower (广州塔)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Locals call it the "Supermodel" for a reason — at 600 meters, it's the tallest structure in Guangdong. Go up to the 450-meter open-air deck for the bubble tram that circles the tower's waist, or try the sky drop ride if heights don't faze you. Time your visit for sunset when the whole city turns golden below, then stay for the light show after dark.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="clear-both hidden md:block"></div>
 </div>

 <div class="flex flex-col md:block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
 <div class="order-2 md:float-left md:mr-8 md:mb-4 md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/shamian-island.jpg" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="order-1">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Shamian Island (沙面岛)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">This tiny river island was the British and French concession in the 1860s, and those European consulate buildings still line the boulevards today. Giant 150-year-old banyan trees shade the pedestrian paths, their roots draped like curtains. It takes about 20 minutes to walk from one end to the other — best done in the late afternoon when the heat drops and the light filters through the leaves.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="clear-both hidden md:block"></div>
 </div>

 <div class="flex flex-col md:block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
 <div class="order-2 md:float-left md:mr-8 md:mb-4 md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/chen-clan-hall.jpg" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="order-1">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Chen Clan Ancestral Hall (陈家祠)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Built in 1894 by the Chen clan as an academy and temple, this complex has 19 halls packed with the finest Lingnan wood carvings you'll find anywhere in China. Every ridge beam and roof peak is covered in ceramic figurines — lions, dragons, immortals. It's now a folk art museum, so you can wander the halls and see the embroidery and pottery up close. Give yourself at least an hour.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="clear-both hidden md:block"></div>
 </div>

 <div class="flex flex-col md:block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 overflow-hidden">
 <div class="order-2 md:float-left md:mr-8 md:mb-4 md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/yuexiu-park-five-rams.jpg" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="order-1">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Yuexiu Park (越秀公园)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">This park sits on a hill that was once a fort for the ancient Nanyue Kingdom, over 2,000 years ago. Inside you'll find the Five Rams Statue (the official symbol of Guangzhou) and the Zhenhai Tower, the oldest standing building in the city — built way back in 1380. Climb the tower for a decent view of the park below. Best on a weekday morning when it's mostly old locals practicing tai chi and playing mahjong.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="clear-both hidden md:block"></div>
 </div>

 <div class="flex flex-col md:block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12 overflow-hidden">
 <div class="order-2 md:float-left md:mr-8 md:mb-4 md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/beijing-road.jpg" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="order-1">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Beijing Road (北京路)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Under the glass panels at your feet, you can see the actual paved streets of the Song, Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties stacked like history layers — that's over 2,000 years of walking routes right beneath your shoes. Above ground, it's a non-stop shopping and snacking strip with fried milk, grilled squid, and bubble tea at every turn. Come after 7pm when the neon lights flicker on and the night market crowd takes over.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="clear-both hidden md:block"></div>
 </div>

 <div class="flex flex-col md:block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12 overflow-hidden">
 <div class="order-2 md:float-left md:mr-8 md:mb-4 md:w-80 lg:w-96 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/grand-buddha-temple.jpg" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="order-1">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Grand Buddha Temple (大佛寺)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The main hall is 36 meters wide but has almost no interior columns — the whole roof is carried by a complex dougong bracket system, and you feel the space open up the moment you step inside. This isn't your typical Cantonese temple with colorful ceramic ridges. Built in 1663 by a Qing prince, it was modeled after imperial Beijing palace architecture, so it has that solemn Forbidden City scale you'd never expect to find in this part of China. Inside sit three 6-meter bronze Buddhas, each weighing 10 tons — Guangdong's largest surviving ancient bronze statues. The pillars are Vietnamese nanmu wood delivered over 350 years ago and still intact. You come for the kind of architectural rarity that simply doesn't exist anywhere else in Guangzhou.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="clear-both hidden md:block"></div>
 </div>

 <h2 id="getting-around" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🚇 Getting Around</h2>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">
 <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Guangzhou's metro has 16 lines and reaches all major attractions. English signs everywhere. Check the <a href="/th/transport/metro-bus/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities</a> for an overview.</li>
 <li><strong>Didi:</strong> Cheap and widely available. A ride across the city center costs about 30–50 RMB. See <a href="/th/transport/didi/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">How to Use Didi in China as a Foreigner</a> for setup and tips.</li>
 <li><strong>Pearl River Ferry:</strong> A scenic way to cross the river. Ferries run regularly between piers on both sides.</li>
 <li><strong>High-speed rail:</strong> Guangzhou South Station connects to Shenzhen (30 min), Hong Kong (1 hour), and Zhuhai/Macau (1 hour). Read the <a href="/th/transport/high-speed-rail/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">How to Book and Ride China High-Speed Rail</a> for booking instructions.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="where-to-stay" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🏨 Where to Stay</h2>
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6"><h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Liwan District (荔湾区) — Best for food lovers</h3><p class="text-gray-700">The heart of old Guangzhou. Stay here for authentic Cantonese restaurants and morning tea houses.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6"><h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Tianhe District (天河区) — Best for modern travelers</h3><p class="text-gray-700">The new CBD has shopping malls, international hotels, and excellent metro connections.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12"><h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Haizhu District (海珠区) — Best for river views</h3><p class="text-gray-700">Across the Pearl River from the city center. Quieter, with great views of the Canton Tower.</p></div>

 <!-- ==================== WHAT TO EAT ==================== -->
 <h2 id="what-to-eat" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🍜 What to Eat</h2>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">If you eat to live in Beijing, you live to eat in Guangzhou. Cantonese cuisine is world-famous for a reason.</p>

 <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-12">
 <!-- Dim Sum -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/dim-sum.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Dim Sum (点心)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">The four classics to order: har gow with their translucent, almost see-through skins wrapping whole shrimp; siu mai topped with orange roe; char siu bao — impossibly fluffy white buns stuffed with sweet barbecued pork; and cheung fun, silky rice noodle rolls drizzled with soy. The ritual is called yum cha (drinking tea), and it happens every morning in dim sum houses across Liwan District. Go early — by 11am the queues stretch out the door.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- White Cut Chicken -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/white-cut-chicken.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">White Cut Chicken — Bai Qie Ji (白切鸡)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">A whole chicken is gently poached, then served cold — and the key is that silky skin with the thin gelatinous layer underneath. Cantonese chefs consider this dish the ultimate test of skill because there's nowhere to hide: the chicken has to be perfect. Dip each piece in the ginger-scallion oil and you'll understand why something so simple can taste so good.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- Roast Goose -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/roast-goose.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Roast Goose — Shao E (烧鹅)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">A whole goose split open, marinated, and roasted over charcoal until the skin turns glass-brittle and the meat stays dark and juicy. It comes with a side of plum dipping sauce that cuts through the richness. This style originates from the Jiangmen area, just west of Guangzhou, and the best roast meat shops in town have been perfecting it for decades.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- Wonton Noodles -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/wonton-noodles.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Wonton Noodles — Yun Tun Mian (云吞面)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">The noodles are thin, springy, and made with alkaline water (the Cantonese "yue-style" noodle). The wontons are plump with whole shrimp and pork. But the real star is the broth — clear, golden, simmered with dried flounder and shrimp roe. It's the kind of bowl that feels restorative.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- Claypot Rice -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/claypot-rice.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Claypot Rice — Bao Zai Fan (煲仔饭)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Raw rice goes into a clay pot, topped with Chinese sausage, cured pork belly, or chicken, then cooked over a charcoal fire. The magic is at the bottom — a layer of crispy, golden rice (the Cantonese version of socarrat) that you scrape up and mix in. Add a splash of soy sauce and give it a good stir before digging in.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- Double Skin Milk -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/double-skin-milk.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Double Skin Milk — Shuang Pi Nai (双皮奶)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">This delicate dessert comes from Shunde, the culinary capital just south of Guangzhou. The name refers to the two layers of skin that form on top as the milk pudding cools — the first one sinks, then a second forms on the surface. It's smooth as custard but much lighter, and it works warm or cold.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- Herbal Tea -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/herbal-tea.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Herbal Tea — Liang Cha (凉茶)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Dark as cola and bitter as medicine — because it is medicine. Liang cha is brewed from 20 or more Chinese herbs, with different blends for different ailments (heatiness, sore throat, indigestion). Go for the full experience and order a "24 flavors" (24味) — it'll make your face scrunch, but locals say that's how you know it's working. Available at dedicated herbal tea shops all over the city.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

 <!-- Claypot Cooking - Jue Jue Bao -->
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
 <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
 <img src="/images/guangzhou/jue-jue-bao.jpg" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
 </div>
 <div class="p-4">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Claypot Cooking — Zhe Zhe Bao (啫啫煲)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">The name comes from the sizzling sound ("zhe-zhe") the claypot makes when hot ingredients hit its surface. Unlike slow-cooked claypot rice, this is high-heat, fast-cook Cantonese cooking at its most dramatic: raw chicken, squid, beef, or eel goes into a pre-heated claypot with fermented black beans, garlic, and ginger, then gets tossed over an open flame for just a few minutes. The edges caramelize, the aroma hits you before the dish even reaches the table, and that first bite has a smoky depth you don't get from stir-frying in a wok. Order one pot per person — they're meant to be eaten piping hot straight from the clay.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>

